QEMU 11.1.1 gdbstub, user mode: gdb's breakpoints change with the other CPUs out of cpu_exec.

paslang's arm64 tests run under QEMU's user mode, built from the signed
11.1.1 release with this patch (scripts/build_qemu.py). Its system call
injections run the program under QEMU's gdb stub (scripts/check_syscalls.py),
and QEMU died of its own SIGSEGV there (paslang P349): 4 runs of 600 of
the handoff injection beside four busy loops on one processor, and a
chain of make check once (QEMU internal SIGSEGV {code=128, addr=(nil)},
or SEGV_MAPERR in systemd-coredump). Under a host gdb with a breakpoint at
die_from_signal, three faults of three were the same:

- a CPU's thread in check_for_breakpoints_slow() (accel/tcg/cpu-exec.c),
  at QTAILQ_FOREACH over cpu->breakpoints, from helper_lookup_tb_ptr()
  in translated code;
- gdb_breakpoint_insert(), gdb_breakpoint_remove() and
  gdb_breakpoint_remove_all() (gdbstub/user.c) change every CPU's list
  (CPU_FOREACH, cpu_breakpoint_insert() and cpu_breakpoint_remove_by_ref(),
  which frees the entry) from the thread of the CPU that stopped, while
  in user mode the other CPUs' threads run on (gdb_handlesig() waits in
  the stopping thread alone) and walk their lists with no lock.

The three now make their changes in an exclusive section
(start_exclusive() and end_exclusive(), cpu-common.c, as gdbserver_fork's
already does), every other CPU out of cpu_exec meanwhile; they run on a
CPU's thread outside cpu_exec, as start_exclusive() asks, and the fork
path, already exclusive, counts the section again; the first changes,
from gdbserver_start() in main() before any CPU runs, have current_cpu
NULL and no other thread, and take no section. With it the handoff
injection ran its loop beside four busy loops without a fault.

--- a/gdbstub/user.c
+++ b/gdbstub/user.c
@@ -788,8 +788,30 @@
 
 /*
  * Break/Watch point helpers
+ *
+ * Every CPU's breakpoint list changes here, from the thread of the CPU
+ * that stopped, while the other threads run on and walk their own lists
+ * in check_for_breakpoints_slow(): each change is made with them out of
+ * cpu_exec, in an exclusive section, so none reads an entry that
+ * cpu_breakpoint_remove_by_ref() frees. The first changes come from
+ * gdbserver_start() in main(), before any CPU runs and with current_cpu
+ * NULL, where start_exclusive() would fault and no list is walked.
  */
 
+static void gdb_breakpoints_begin(void)
+{
+    if (current_cpu) {
+        start_exclusive();
+    }
+}
+
+static void gdb_breakpoints_end(void)
+{
+    if (current_cpu) {
+        end_exclusive();
+    }
+}
+
 int gdb_breakpoint_insert(CPUState *cs, GdbBreakpointType type,
                           vaddr addr, vaddr len)
 {
@@ -799,12 +821,14 @@
     switch (type) {
     case GDB_BREAKPOINT_SW:
     case GDB_BREAKPOINT_HW:
+        gdb_breakpoints_begin();
         CPU_FOREACH(cpu) {
             err = cpu_breakpoint_insert(cpu, addr, BP_GDB, NULL);
             if (err) {
                 break;
             }
         }
+        gdb_breakpoints_end();
         return err;
     default:
         /* user-mode doesn't support watchpoints */
@@ -821,12 +845,14 @@
     switch (type) {
     case GDB_BREAKPOINT_SW:
     case GDB_BREAKPOINT_HW:
+        gdb_breakpoints_begin();
         CPU_FOREACH(cpu) {
             err = cpu_breakpoint_remove(cpu, addr, BP_GDB);
             if (err) {
                 break;
             }
         }
+        gdb_breakpoints_end();
         return err;
     default:
         /* user-mode doesn't support watchpoints */
@@ -836,7 +862,9 @@
 
 void gdb_breakpoint_remove_all(CPUState *cs)
 {
+    gdb_breakpoints_begin();
     cpu_breakpoint_remove_all(cs, BP_GDB);
+    gdb_breakpoints_end();
 }
 
